      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p17_b12",
      "text": "To demonstrate the pervasiveness of language bias across diverse datasets and model architectures, we provide additional\ntraining dynamics in Figure 8. Specifically, we plot the trajectories of Language Bias and Reward during DPO for (a)\nLLaVA-v1.5-7B trained on the VLFeedback dataset and (b) Qwen2.5-VL-3B trained on the RLHF-V dataset. The definitions\nfor all metrics remain consistent with those in Figure 3 (b).\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        617.81,
        542.11,
        663.67
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2471,
        2168,
        2654
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.0,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 416,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p17_b13",
      "text": "The results confirm that the emergence of language bias is a common phenomenon in DPO training. However, for the more\nadvanced Qwen2.5-VL-3B model (Figure 8 (b)), we observe a more pronounced gap between the multimodal reward (R)\nand the text-only bias (B) for chosen responses compared to LLaVA-v1.5. This indicates that while language bias persists,\nthe more advanced architecture exhibits a relatively lower degree of reliance on pure language priors.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        671.68,
        542.69,
        717.47
      ],
